# Phase 18 ProcessQueue expiry semantics and current-revision P18-V02 refresh — 2026-10-21T16:20:10Z

Branch: `phase/18-phase-18-compatibility-closure`
Code revision: `9147a902` (`fix(consumer): match expired message cleanup semantics`)
Pinned Java source: `efa1be59d6fc747ad220f036c86030f05f0419f9`.

## Source finding and change

Pinned `ProcessQueue.cleanExpiredMsg` reads the oldest cached message's
`CONSUME_START_TIME` and invokes `Long.parseLong` before entering its send-back
exception handler. A malformed non-empty timestamp therefore raises
`NumberFormatException`, makes no send-back attempt, and leaves the message in
the queue. The concurrent-service scheduled wrapper catches and logs any
`Throwable`; the exception aborts that sweep and the next scheduled tick runs
normally. When send-back itself fails, the direct method catches the failure
and continues its original queue-size loop, retrying the same head at most 16
times.

Rust queue entries now retain missing, parsed, and malformed timestamp states.
The public direct cleanup returns `ExpiredMessageCleanupError` for malformed
input; the scheduled owner logs that error and defers the next attempt until
its next tick. Direct send-back failures retry the same unchanged head through
the original bounded loop. The pinned Java fixture now records malformed
timestamp exception class, empty attempts, and unchanged queue count/bytes.
Rust compares both direct and scheduled error paths, direct retry behavior,
scheduled retry cap, and prior success/orderly cases.

